From 5238a9f02101792d0995b069ae34361d71b6220d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HampterDev <131729366+HampterDev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 09:02:07 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Open only http and https targets from text-viewer URL links

Both <URL> link handlers, on_link in text_viewer_links.c and
ui_tag_text_viewer_on_url_link in tag_text_viewer.c, pass the link
target straight to ShellExecuteA with "open". The target comes from
<URL>...<INFO>target</INFO></URL> markup in server-supplied text (NPC
dialog, quest panes, message boxes), so a server could make a click
start a local or UNC executable, open a file: URL, or invoke any
registered protocol handler such as ms-msdt: or search-ms:.

Add ui_open_url_link, used by both handlers. It opens a target only if
it begins with http:// or https://, compared case-insensitively for
ASCII, with nothing before the scheme, at least one character after
the //, and no embedded NUL. Any other target stays unopened: the link
label is still shown and clicking it does nothing.

The data is already a C string: link arguments are stored by
owned_text_assign, which writes a NUL at the view's size, and a missing
argument is the empty literal. No copy is needed; the embedded-NUL check
makes the bytes that are checked the same as the string the shell
receives.

Validation: the new test_url_link runs both extracted handlers with
ShellExecuteA stubbed. http://x, HTTPS://example.com/path?q and a
mixed-case scheme open; file:///C:/x, a drive path, a UNC path, ms-msdt:,
search-ms:, javascript:, http:/x, http://, https://, an empty target,
" http://x", an embedded NUL and missing arguments do not. A target
taken from part of a longer text opens exactly that part. The test fails
on the previous code. test_tag_text_viewer now checks that its item
argument "62" no longer opens and that an https link does. Release build
without warnings; the full behavioral suite passed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
